\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $p$ be a prime and $\zeta$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity in a field of characteristic zero. Chebotarev's theorem asserts that every square submatrix of the discrete Fourier transform matrix $(\zeta^{jk})_{j,k\in\Zp}$ is nonsingular. We give a complete, self-contained development along the lines of Frenkel's polynomial argument, together with a systematic derivation of its analytic and combinatorial consequences. We prove: the theorem itself and its quantitative core, namely that the integer polynomial $P(X)=\det\big((1+X)^{a_ib_j}\big)$ vanishes at the origin to order exactly $\binom{n}{2}$ with $\binom{n}{2}$-th coefficient $\det(a_i^{k})\cdot\det\binom{b_i}{k}$ prime to $p$; a converse showing that every composite modulus admits an explicit singular $2\times2$ minor, so that nonsingularity of all square submatrices characterises primality; a rectangular full-row-rank form; the prime-order uncertainty principle $\#\supp f+\#\supp\hf\ge p+1$, its sharpness on \emph{every} prescribed support and the one-dimensionality of the extremal kernels; exact recovery of $k$-sparse signals from an \emph{arbitrary} set of $2k$ Fourier coefficients, with a matching negative result at $2k-1$; and a Fourier-analytic proof of the Cauchy--Davenport theorem obtained by convolving two extremal functions. Algorithms extracted from the proofs, with complexities, are also given.
\end{abstract}

